[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"OCP: Core Concept and Extension via Polymorphism"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Open-Closed Principle (OCP) is the second of the SOLID principles of object-oriented design. Originally coined by Bertrand Meyer and later adapted by Robert C. Martin, OCP states that software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension, but closed for modification."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Core Concept"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Being 'open for extension' means that the behavior of the module can be extended as the requirements of the application change. Being 'closed for modification' means that extending the behavior of a module does not entail making changes to the source code of the module itself. Achieving this balance typically relies on abstraction and polymorphism."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The Anti-Pattern: Violation of OCP"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a reporting system where a ReportGenerator class checks the type of report to determine how to format it. Every time a new report type is added, this class must be modified."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"class ReportGenerator {
    public void generateReport(Report report) {
        if (report.getType().equals("PDF")) {
            // Generate PDF logic
        } else if (report.getType().equals("CSV")) {
            // Generate CSV logic
        }
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"This design violates OCP because adding an HTML report requires modifying the ReportGenerator class, risking regressions in existing functionality."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Extension via Polymorphism"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To adhere to OCP, we can use polymorphism. Instead of conditional statements checking types, we define an interface that encapsulates the varying behavior. Each new report type implements this interface, allowing the core system to remain untouched."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public interface ReportFormatter {
    void format();
}

public class PdfReportFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public void format() {
        // Generate PDF logic
    }
}

public class CsvReportFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public void format() {
        // Generate CSV logic
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring the Consumer Class"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"With polymorphism in place, the ReportGenerator no longer needs to know about specific report formats. It depends only on the abstraction."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class ReportGenerator {
    public void generateReport(ReportFormatter formatter) {
        formatter.format();
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open for Extension: New formats can be added by creating new classes implementing ReportFormatter."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Closed for Modification: ReportGenerator remains completely unchanged when new formats are introduced."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging polymorphism and interfaces, OCP helps build maintainable, decoupled, and scalable software systems where new features are added by writing new code rather than altering tested code."}]
